From 9a171995b0970c4ca2b22c6efc7b76b1bf39360c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 22:53:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] l2r-local: bug 26: no llvm.assume(launder(p) == p) after an
 invariant-group launder

When a unique cell is reused for a new record, the new record's tag and
field stores carry !invariant.group like the old record's, so the token
is laundered first (token.launder lowers to
llvm.launder.invariant.group): the stores go through a fresh pointer and
LLVM does not take the old values for the new ones. The lowering then
added `llvm.assume(icmp eq launder(p), p)`, deliberately, "to recover
alias/value propagation after the launder". GVN and EarlyCSE use that
equality to replace the laundered pointer by `p` (on one repro, GVN alone
moves four !invariant.group stores back onto the old pointer). That undoes
the launder: the new record's stores and the old record's loads and
stores now share one pointer and one invariant group, which tells LLVM
that the cell's tag and fields never change. In a loop that rebuilds one
cell in place, LICM then hoisted the tag stores out of the loop (opt
-opt-bisect-limit lands on LICM in that loop in all three cases bisected),
and the result was a wrong value, or the computation folded to a constant.

    enum S { A(u8, u16), B(bool), C(u64) }
    fn step(x : S, s : u64) -> S { match x {
        S::A(a, b) => { S::B{s % 2 == 1} },
        S::B(f) => { S::A{(s % 251) as u8, (s % 65521) as u16} },
        S::C(w) => { S::C{w + s} } } }
    fn iter(x : S, n : u64, s : u64) -> S {
        if n == 0 { x } else { iter(step(x, s), n - 1, (s * 33 + 10) % 1000003) } }

sum(iter(A{1, 2}, 6, 153)) must be A{250, 9648} (25009648). At -O
aggressive, with or without lean2rr's flags (--no-pack-record-members
--reuse-across-call), it was B(false) (2). It was right at -O none and
-O default. The LLVM IR rrc hands to LLVM was right under clang -O0/-O1,
and wrong after the O2/O3 pipeline. Deleting the icmp/assume pairs from
that IR made it, and five fuzzer programs that failed the same way,
right under rrc's own pipeline.

Emit no assume after the launder, in the token.launder lowering and in
the token.realloc lowering (which launders the reallocated pointer and
had the same assume). The comment in the token.launder lowering now says
why. token_ops.mlir and rc_create_fused_lowering.mlir checked for the
assume; they now check that none follows the launder.

Found by differential fuzzing of the bug 1, 2 and 8 patches (random record
and enum shapes against a reference evaluator). Test:
tests/integration/frontend/reuse_launder_loop.
